// The `#[foreign]` mark. It lives here rather than in the compiler's own tier
// because its users are outside the compiler: a language plugin generating
// marked SNGL to describe a foreign API, a platform package naming its host
// types.
import ir "sngl:internal/ir"

// Records what a declaration corresponds to outside SNGL: the import path it
// comes from and its name there, written the way an import line writes them.
// A struct field names its name alone, `#[foreign(name="Title")]`, since a
// field has no package of its own.
//
// The mark imports nothing, resolves nothing and validates nothing — a
// plugin's output is trusted — and it never confers type identity: several
// SNGL types can map to one target-language type, so two declarations naming
// the same foreign thing stay two types. Only a scheme importer's own origin
// unifies declarations.
//
// A marked declaration is still the program's own: a backend emits it, so the
// name is a name to spell alongside that declaration and never a reference
// redirecting to one the backend did not emit. Struct, struct field and
// function are the forms that carry it; the others refuse it. The flags that
// may follow the name are `ir.ForeignFlag`.
func foreign(path string = "", name string = "", flags list[ir.ForeignFlag] = []) ir.Macro

// Names the C identifier a declaration *is*: a function to call, or a type to
// spell. The declaration itself is emitted by nobody.
//
// Language-neutral on purpose. C is the one ABI several targets can reach, and
// a platform built on a C library has no business naming a SNGL language --
// `sngl:platform/gtk4` describes cairo with this and nothing Go-specific, so
// any backend that can call C can serve it. Today that is the Go one, which
// renders a call as cgo. A `string`, `int`, `float` or `bool` argument crosses
// through the conversion `sngl:x/c` declares for its parameter's type, written
// in before any backend sees the call.
//
// The name is spelled as C spells it, and each language adds what it needs to
// reach it: a function is its identifier (`cairo_arc`), and a type is a C type
// (`cairo_t *`, `cairo_line_cap_t`, `const char *`) -- a name, `const` written
// first, and pointers. An array or a function pointer is not a type this
// spells.
//
// `cast` declares a conversion rather than a function: a bodyless function of
// one parameter whose name is the C type it converts to, which is the C type
// its return type declares. `#[cnative("cairo_line_cap_t", cast)] const func toLineCap(v int) LineCap` makes a number one, and a pointer type makes a
// pointer, or a node, one.
//
// An alias carries it too, naming a C typedef of a type SNGL has:
// `#[cnative("UINT")] struct UINT = int` is an `int` wherever it is written,
// and an argument for a C function's parameter written with it crosses cast
// to `UINT` rather than as a C `int`. An alias of `string` names a pointer to
// 16-bit units, which the string crosses as UTF-16: `sngl:x/c`'s `utf16`.
//
// A struct declaring fields is a C struct the program holds as its own, with
// that layout: `#[cnative("RECT")] struct RECT { left int ... }`, each field
// named as C names it and typed as a scalar, an alias of a C typedef or
// another C struct. A call converts it field by field, and a function's
// `ref[RECT]` parameter is a pointer C fills, which the call writes back to
// the variable `&r` names.
//
// A function's `func` parameter is a callback, which C calls through a
// function pointer and the `void *` it hands back: the parameter
// `sngl:x/c`'s `#[context]` marks, which no caller passes.
//
// Struct, function and alias are the forms that carry it.
func cnative(name string, flags list[CNativeFlag] = []) ir.Macro

// What a `#[cnative]` function is beyond its name.
enum CNativeFlag {
    // The function is a conversion to the C type its name spells, which its
    // return type declares, rather than a C function to call.
    cast
}
